titre$Key to the omphalinoid species- Gro Gulden
com$Gro Gulden A preliminary guide to the macromycetes in the Finse area / GF 2011

1,35$Omphalinoid genera 
1,2 $Fruitbody whitish or with warm colours (cream, yellow, ochre, orange, pale beige, brown to flesh-brown) or with ± green or lilac parts, pileus never scaly
1,17 $Dull, nut-brown to very dark species (grey-brown, grey, blackish), pileus smooth or scaly
2,0 $Fruitbody ± green $Omphalina chlorocyanea
2,3 $Without green colours
3,4 $Fruitbody yellow to orange
3,10 $Fruitbody white to cream, beige, or brown
4,5 $Pileus ochre-orange to rusty-brown
4,6 $Pileus yellow
5,0 $Clamps absent; on burns (often with Marchantia) and peat, and among bryophytes in pioneer vegetation $Loreleia postii
5,0 $Clamps present; in rich fen $Omphalina galericolor
6,7 $Associated wit green algae (seen as a thin, dark green crust at stipe base or as lichen scales), clamps absent
6,8 $Not associated with green algae, clamps present
7,0 $Lichen scales at base, stipe distinctly pubescent $Lichenomphalia hudsoniana
7,0 $Dark green algal crust at stipe base, stipe matt, smooth $Lichenomphalia alpina
8,0 $Fruitbody viscid and shiny when fresh, pileus not papillate, not associated with algae $Hygrocybe citrinopallens/lilacina
8,9 $Fruitbody fatty-shiny (not viscid) or matt (tomented-scurfy-subscaly), pileus papillate
9,0 $Fruitbody slender with thin, pubescent stipe, cystidia large, numerous $Rickenella fibula
9,0 $Fruitbody not particularly slender, stipe smooth, cystidia absent $Sphagnomphalia brevibasidiata
10,0 $Pileus vinaceous brown to flesh-brown, drying to leathery brown. Pileus 1.5-3 cm wide $Omphalia rivulicola
10,11 $Pileus white, cream, beige to brown, mostly smaller than above choice
11,12 $Fruitbody slender, stipe tall, thin (up to 1 mm thick) and pubescent (cystidia), clamps present
11,13 $Fruitbody not particularly slender, stipe 1-2 mm thick, clamps present or absent
12,0 $Stipe apex violet, pileus grey-brown $Rickenella swartzii
12,0 $Stipe evenly beige like the pileus $Rickenella sp.
13,14 $With  green algae at stipe base (also when growing on Sphagnum), clamps absent
13,15 $Without algae at stipe base, clamps present
14,0 $Stipe smooth, shiny, apex persistently brown, fruitbody brown when young, gradually becoming paler, ending ± cream-coloured $Lichenomphalia umbellifera
14,0 $Stipe pubescent in entire length, pileus not fading to whitish $Lichenomphalia velutina
15,0 $Pileus and stipe white, pileus not hygrophanous, lamellae beige to ochre, spores warted, cyanophilic $Ripartites metrodii
15,0 $Pileus brown, medium dark, spores smooth, acyanophilic $
16,0 $Sphagnicolous $Omphalina philonotis
16,0 $Not sphagnicolous $Omphalina kuehneri
17,18 $Carbonicolous, growing on old burns,
17,19 $Not growing on burns
18,0 $Cuticle gelatinous-viscid $Myxomphalia maura
18,0 $Cuticle not gelatinous $Lyophyllum anthracophilum
19,20 $Spores globose and warted; growing in Vaccinium-Empetrum-lichen heaths
19,21 $Spores smooth; only occasionally growing in heaths
20,0 $Pileus dark brown, deeply umbonate, not striate $Omphaliaster borealis
20,0 $Pileus grey, plano-depressed, translucently striate $Omphaliaster asterosporus
21,22 $In bogs
21,23 $On 'black soils', e.g. silty river flats, old tracks, late snow-beds, road-sides, often moist
22,0 $Pileus smooth, blackish brown $Omphalina oniscus
22,0 $Pileus ± felted-small scaly, medium dark brown, nut-brown $Omphalina philonotis
23,24 $Stipe pubescent to velutinous (lens!), basidia 2- or 4-spored
23,28 $Stipe smooth to minutely scabrous, basidia 4-spored
24,25 $With green algae at stipe base, clamps absent
24,26 $Without green algae, clamps present; basidia 4-spored
25,0 $Basidia 2-spored $Lichenomphalia velutina
25,0 $Basidia 4-spored $Lichenomphalia pararustica
26,0 $Cheilocystidia present, stipe minutely rugulose, pruinose at apex $Omphalina umbratilis
26,27 $Cheilocystidia absent, stipe distinctly pubescent (lens!)
27,0 $Stipe 1-3 mm thick, densely white tomentose from base upwards,  eccentric, lamellae  forked and thickish $Arrhenia griseopallida
27,0 $Stipe pubescent in whole length, 1-1.5 mm thick, central, lamellae not thickish and forked $Omphalina velutipes
28,0 $Spores 8-11 x 4.0-6 µm, broadly ellipsoid-lacrymoid (broader towards apex) $Omphalina obscurata
28,0 $Spores smaller, 7.2-8.4 x 4.8-5.5 µm, ellipsoid amygdaloid (narrowing towards apex ) $Omphalina obatra
35,0$Fruitbodies of the omphalinoid species are small with decurrent lamellae. Generally the pileus is depressed to umbilicate and hygrophanous, with distinct translucent, radial striation when moist. The stipe is thin and generally solid, ca 1 to 3 mm thick. Morphological studies during recent decades have led to segregation of species groups from the old genus Omphalina and erection of new genera such as Lichenomphalia (as Phytoconis), Omphaliaster and Rickenella. Combined morphological and molecular studies have later led to confirmation of these and erection of some more segregate genera (Redhead et al. (2002a,b). These authors also included all the grey to blackish former Omhalina species in Arrhenia and left only the red-brown species in Omphalina. In the present study I have accepted the new genera, but kept the grey-blackish species in Omphalina, this in order to better pursue the grouping in ‘omphalinoid’ species, ‘collybioid’ species, ‘mycenoid’ species, etc ///Included genera: Lichenomphalia, Loreleia, Myxomphalia, Omphaliaster, Rickenella, Ripartites and Sphagnomphalia. One species of Lyophyllum with somewhat decurrent lamellae is treated under the collybioid genera.
